#### Functions with Keyword Args
<hr class="section-rule lvl-4">

!!! danger "Advanced Topic"
    This is an advanced topic and safe for new users to ignore!

You might have noticed that internal functions for stuff like [import statements]({{_}}/bridge) also support named arguments.  Building your own functions that support kwargs looks like this:

A single call to the `mk.unpack.kwargs` macro unpacks one key, given the input, a key name, and a default; or, with no third argument, it unpacks a whole space-separated spec at once.  Passing a spec like `shape color=default quoted='default data'` sets `kwargs_shape`, `kwargs_color`, and `kwargs_quoted` (single quotes keep spaces in a default).

{{site.embed_mirror('demos/kwarg-parsing-3.mk', 'demos/cmk/kwarg-parsing-3.cmk',
    github=github, default='left',
    see_also=_+'/cmk/compiler/#bind-declarations', see_also_text='CMK bind-declarations')}}


```bash {.cli_example}
$ ./demos/kwarg-parsing-3.mk
```
```bash {.cli_output}
{{bash('./demos/kwarg-parsing-3.mk').strip()}}
```

The positional counterpart is `mk.unpack.arg(<index>, <optional_default>)`, which returns one comma-separated field of the stem, so reading index `2` yields `b` from a `target/a,b,c` call.  For a different delimiter, use the general `mk.unpack.any(<index>, <delim>, <optional_default>)` (of which `mk.unpack.arg` is the comma-fixed shorthand): a `/`-separated `target/a/b/c` reads index `2` as `b`, or index `2-` for the tail `b/c`.

